@page "/grid"

<PageTitle>Grid and layouts · Bortable</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Grid and layouts</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        The component doesn't care about layout - point it at a CSS grid container and you get a sortable grid, at a
        row of chips and you get a horizontal list, at a right-to-left container and it sorts right to left. The list
        is measured as it is laid out, so the same component serves them all.
    </p>
</div>

<DemoCode />

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> A grid</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The list's <code>Class</code> makes it a CSS grid (<code>display: grid</code> with
        <code>auto-fill</code> columns). Drag the squares to rearrange them: they sort across a row and from row to row.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <Bortable @bind-Items="_items" TItem="int" Id="grid-list"
                  Class="demo-grid" GhostClass="demo-ghost">
            <ItemTemplate Context="n">
                <div class="grid-square">@n</div>
            </ItemTemplate>
        </Bortable>

        <p class="demo-order"><strong>Order:</strong> <span id="grid-order">@string.Join(", ", _items)</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> A horizontal list</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        A row of tags, laid out with <code>display: flex</code> and wrapping when the row is full. No
        <code>Direction</code> is set: <code>Auto</code> sees the first two items side by side and measures the list
        left to right.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <Bortable @bind-Items="_tags" TItem="string" Id="layout-tags" AriaLabel="Tags"
                  Class="demo-strip" ItemClass="demo-chip" GhostClass="demo-ghost">
            <ItemTemplate Context="tag">@tag</ItemTemplate>
        </Bortable>

        <p class="demo-order"><strong>Tags:</strong> <span id="layout-tags-order">@string.Join(", ", _tags)</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Right to left</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The same row under <code>dir="rtl"</code>: the first step is on the right. An attribute the component does not
        know as a parameter - <code>dir</code> here, an <code>aria-*</code> or <code>data-*</code> attribute elsewhere -
        is rendered on the list's container as it is (<code>AdditionalAttributes</code>). The list is measured in its
        own order, so dragging toward the left moves an item forward, and from the keyboard <kbd>←</kbd> moves it to the
        next place.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <Bortable @bind-Items="_steps" TItem="string" Id="layout-rtl" AriaLabel="Steps" dir="rtl"
                  Class="demo-strip" ItemClass="demo-chip tinted" GhostClass="demo-ghost">
            <ItemTemplate Context="step">@step</ItemTemplate>
        </Bortable>

        <p class="demo-order"><strong>Steps:</strong> <span id="layout-rtl-order">@string.Join(", ", _steps)</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Try it</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Drag square <em>1</em> onto square <em>7</em>, two rows down: it sorts across rows, not only along one.</li>
        <li>Drag <em>css</em> to the start of the tags: the others slide right to make room.</li>
        <li>In the right-to-left row, drag <em>Step 1</em> to the far left: it becomes the last step.</li>
        <li>
            From the keyboard the arrows follow the layout: <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> along a row, <kbd>↑</kbd>
            <kbd>↓</kbd> from row to row of the grid.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Good to know</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>
            The hit test is two-dimensional, so a grid sorts in every direction. <code>Direction</code> decides only which
            way an item's swap zones run; the <a href="thresholds">swap thresholds</a> page shows them.
        </li>
        <li>
            Lists reversed with <code>flex-direction: row-reverse</code> or <code>column-reverse</code> are measured in
            their own order too, like right-to-left ones.
        </li>
        <li>
            The item wrappers are the direct children of the container, so the grid or the flex row lays them out:
            <code>ItemClass</code> styles them, or - as in the grid - the template inside them.
        </li>
        <li>For lists inside the items of another list, see <a href="nested">nested sortables</a>.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

@code {
    private IList<int> _items = Enumerable.Range(1, 16).ToList();

    private IList<string> _tags = new List<string> { "blazor", "dotnet", "drag-and-drop", "sortable", "css", "a11y" };

    private IList<string> _steps = new List<string> { "Step 1", "Step 2", "Step 3", "Step 4", "Step 5" };
}
